        "SWAYNE: Not really, I still see my younger brother. My mum went in 2018. [shrugs] Cancer. Long time coming, that one. I looked after her, best I could, at the end.",
        "FERRIS: Look, I know you've got a bit of a history and I don't want to go over old ground unnecessarily but, just briefly, can I ask about your record?",
        "SWAYNE: It's alright. I know what's on there. Drugs mostly. Started young. Stopped for a bit after the second prison sentence but started using again after my mum. Crack. Brown when I can get it. Whatever's about, really. Since I was a teenager really. Tried a couple of times to get help. Did courses in prison and then when I got out. Never stuck.",
        "FERRIS: Just so we understand what a day looks like for you. Can you tell me what you did the morning of the 29th?",
        "SWAYNE: [thinks] Slept late. Watched telly a bit. Went to the shop for milk and baccy, I think. Normal sort of day.",
        "FERRIS: And the afternoon?",
        "SWAYNE: I was in the flat. Started using. Probably 1 or 2 o'clock, I get up late. And that would've been crack, and I had some beers.",
        "FERRIS: Was anyone else there?",
        "SWAYNE: No.",
        "FERRIS: Ok, do you remember what you were wearing?",
        "SWAYNE: Not really, probably this [gestures to his current outfit] — blue tracksuit bottoms and a white and blue sweater. Probably put a coat on to go to the shops. Same dark blue puffer jacket I've brought today. It's the only one I've got.",
        "FERRIS: Ok, so did anything change in the evening. Did you go out again at all?",
        "SWAYNE: No, I was in all night. Didn't go anywhere.",
        "FERRIS: You're sure about that. We've got a witness who saw you on Bamford Street at around 8:30.",
        "SWAYNE: They're wrong. I didn't go out.",
        "FERRIS: They described your clothes and appearance that day pretty accurately. Are you sure you didn't go out? Can I ask you to think back carefully?",
        "SWAYNE: [silence] [pause] I suppose... I was probably going for a walk.",
        "FERRIS: Okay, this witness says they saw you entering number 42 on Bamford Street. Carl Porterhouse's flat.",
        "SWAYNE: [silence] [pause] I don't remember going out. But I know that Carl owed me some money so maybe I went round to grab it?",
        "FERRIS: What was your relationship with Carl?",
        "SWAYNE: Acquaintance. Dunno, not long, maybe a year? Not really [well]. He'd come round the house sometimes. We'd have a chat. He's sort of my generation, lived in the area too, so we got on ok. He wanted a bit of cash for crack or whatever and I said he could have it. He seemed alright.",
        "FERRIS: Ok, and on the 29th you went to his house and got your money back.",
        "SWAYNE: Yeah. [pause] I think so, I dunno, like I said I don't remember going out. If I was just getting my money probably not long. We might have had a chat.",
        "FERRIS: We've had forensic results back from the property. There's a lot of you in that flat, Colin — not the kind of trace you'd leave dropping something at the door and walking off.",
